  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-e1b0847c Web logs overview\"**\n- Six panels: total requests, unique client IPs, requests over time (line), requests by response code (bar), requests by file extension (pie), and 5xx responses (ES|QL metric).\n- I set its time range to 2026-10-01 through 2026-12-05 so it covers all the data. The default would be the last 7 days, which would likely show little or nothing.\n- Dashboard ID: `eff-e1b0847c-web-logs-overview-78b297f96609`\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-e1b0847c 5xx spike\"**\n- Type: Elasticsearch query, runs every 1m, looks back 5m, fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Query: `response.keyword` starts with `5` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, using `timestamp` as the time field.\n- Tagged `EFF-e1b0847c`. Status is enabled, with the rule ID `e7e34709-b3e8-48d7-9384-be3b1ae7ac41`.\n\n**What the data looks like**\n- 14,074 requests from 1,001 distinct IPs, average 5.6 KB per response.\n- Responses: 200 (12,832), 404 (801), 503 (441). The only 5xx code is 503, so the rule is effectively a 503 counter.\n- Timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03 at roughly 230 requests per day. That is past today (2026-10-12), so the data is partly in the future.\n\n**Things to know**\n- The rule will probably not fire soon. The data is spread across the whole range, so the last 5 minutes before today may have few or no 5xx documents. I did not confirm this with a count of the last-5-minute window, because my check returned an ambiguous result.\n- I did not verify that the rule has run successfully yet. The status is still `pending`, and I did not wait for a first evaluation.",
